Yes. For your specific goal (Windows reverse engineering → vulnerability research → exploit development), I would make the pointer study 2 weeks, not 1. One week is enough to understand syntax, but not enough to build the memory model you need for RE/VR.

The book

Primary book

Understanding and Using C Pointers — Richard Reese



You don't need to read every page. Focus on the pointer-related chapters covering:



Pointer basics / memory addresses

Pointer operators (&, *)

Pointer arithmetic

Pointers and arrays

Pointers and strings

Pointers and structures

Pointers to pointers

Function pointers

Dynamic memory / pointers with malloc() and free()

Common pointer mistakes / pointer-related bugs

Supporting book

C Programming: A Modern Approach — K. N. King



Use this when Reese assumes C knowledge you don't have. Don't read the entire book right now.

What you actually need to know

For your exploit-development path, your target isn't "know pointers."



Your target is:

Given a C program, understand exactly what objects exist in memory, where pointers point, what memory gets read/written, and what happens when those pointers become invalid.

