Your 2-week plan

Assuming ~4 hours/day.

Week 1 — Build the mental model

Day 1 — Memory + pointers

Study



Memory addresses

Variables

&

*

Dereferencing

Pointer declaration

Pointer types

NULL



Code

int x = 10;
int *p = &x;

You should understand:

x   → 10
&x  → address of x
p   → address of x
*p  → 10

Exercise: Draw this on paper.

Day 2 — Pointer arithmetic

Study:

p + 1
p - 1
p++
p--

Understand why:

char *
int *
long *

can move different numbers of bytes.



Lab: Traverse an integer array using only a pointer.

Day 3 — Arrays + pointers

Study:

arr
&arr
&arr[0]
arr[i]
*(arr + i)

Make sure you can explain why:

arr[i]

is related to:

*(arr + i)

Lab: Print the address of every array element.

Day 4 — Strings + buffers

Study:

char *
char []
'\0'
strlen()
sizeof()

Understand the difference between:

buffer capacity
vs
string length

Then learn conceptually why incorrect bounds can cause:

buffer overflow
out-of-bounds write
out-of-bounds read

Day 5 — Structures

Study:

struct
struct *
.
->

Also learn:



memory layout

alignment

padding

sizeof(struct)



Example:

struct User {
    int id;
    char *name;
};

Then inspect the addresses of the members.



This is very important for RE.

Day 6 — Pointer to pointer

Study:

int **p;

Don't memorize it.



Draw:

p
│
▼
address
│
▼
integer

Then understand why pointer-to-pointer is used.

Day 7 — Week 1 assessment

Don't learn new material.



Build a small program containing:

array
struct
pointer
pointer arithmetic
string

Then:



Compile it.

Debug it.

Print addresses.

Draw the memory layout.

Explain every pointer.

Week 1 goal

By the end:

You should understand pointers as addresses pointing to objects in memory—not merely know the syntax.

Week 2 — Deep knowledge for exploit development

This is where I recommend going deeper.

Day 8 — Heap + dynamic memory

Study:

malloc()
calloc()
realloc()
free()

Understand:

heap allocation
 ↓
returned pointer
 ↓
memory access
 ↓
free

Then study:



dangling pointers

use-after-free

double-free

invalid free



Use a debugger to observe what happens.

Day 9 — Stack + pointers

Study:

stack
stack variables
local arrays
function parameters
return values

Understand how pointers reference stack objects.



Then connect this to:

buffer
 ↓
memory corruption
 ↓
control data

Don't worry about building exploits yet.

Day 10 — Function pointers

Study:

int (*fp)(int);

Understand:

function
 ↓
address
 ↓
function pointer
 ↓
indirect call

Then connect it to concepts you'll encounter later:

callbacks
virtual functions
vtables
indirect calls
control-flow integrity

Day 11 — Pointer bugs

Now deliberately study the security consequences.

Learn deeply:

Buffer overflow
Out-of-bounds read
Out-of-bounds write
Use-after-free
Double-free
Dangling pointer
NULL dereference
Uninitialized pointer

For each one answer:



What is the bug?

What memory is affected?

Why does it happen?

What does the crash look like?

What could an attacker potentially control?



That's much more useful than memorizing definitions.

Day 12 — C → Assembly connection

This is the most important day for your long-term goal.



Take simple C:

int x = 10;
int *p = &x;
*p = 20;

Compile it.



Then inspect the assembly.



Your objective is to understand:

C
 ↓
compiler
 ↓
assembly
 ↓
register
 ↓
memory address
 ↓
memory operation

Start recognizing instructions such as:

mov
lea
cmp
test
add
sub
call
ret

You don't need to master assembly yet.



You're building the bridge.

Day 13 — Debugging pointers

Use a debugger and inspect:

registers
stack
memory
pointers
addresses

For each pointer, ask:

Where is the pointer stored?
What value does it contain?
What address does it point to?
What is located there?
Is that memory valid?
Is it readable?
Is it writable?

This is exactly the thinking you'll eventually use during vulnerability research.

